(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-8-cytopenias-hemochromatosis-quiz"]={"title":"Cytopenias and Hemochromatosis Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 8","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 8","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Iron accumulation in the pituitary in hemochromatosis can lead to which disorder?","choices":["Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","Primary testicular failure","Cardiomyopathy","Skin bronzing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Iron accumulation can affect the pituitary and lead to hypogonadotropic hypogonadism: decreased libido, impotence or amenorrhea, with small testicular size, gynecomastia and reduced body hair on examination. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"What level of fetal hemoglobin seems to be the threshold for decreased clinical severity of sickle cell disease or beta thalassemia?","choices":["Less than 1%","2-4%","10-20%","60-70%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fetal hemoglobin is normally less than 1 percent. In hereditary persistence of fetal hemoglobin, expression is variable, but 10 to 20 percent HbF seems to be the threshold for decreased severity of sickle cell disease or beta thalassemia. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"When is referral to hematology recommended in iron deficiency anemia?","choices":["Any gastrointestinal bleed found on workup","Heavy menstrual loss as the identified cause","Constipation that limits oral iron adherence","No response to oral iron or unclear diagnosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Referral to hematology is recommended if the diagnosis is not confirmed or the patient is unresponsive to oral iron therapy. Otherwise treatment means treating the underlying cause and replacing iron. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemia carries an increased risk of which complication?","choices":["Iron overload","Acute chest syndrome","Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","Venous thromboembolism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Autoimmune hemolytic anemia is associated with an increased risk of venous thromboembolism, so consider a D-dimer and bilateral leg ultrasound for deep vein thrombosis or pulmonary embolism, give prophylaxis to inpatients and consider it in outpatients. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 63, 73, 102"},{"q":"What is the defining diagnostic feature of sideroblastic anemia?","choices":["Basophilic stippling on smear","A dimorphic peripheral smear","A raised serum ferritin","Ring sideroblasts in the marrow"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A bone marrow aspirate showing ring sideroblasts is the defining feature: iron-containing mitochondria form a basophilic ring around the nucleus. The blood shows variable microcytosis and hypochromia, a dimorphic smear and excess body iron. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Below which hemoglobin is erythropoietin supplementation an approved indication in anemia of chronic disease?","choices":["7 g/dL","8 g/dL","10 g/dL","12 g/dL"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Erythropoietin supplementation (epoetin alfa) is indicated for a hemoglobin less than 10 g/dL, in anemia due to rheumatoid arthritis, inflammatory bowel disease, hepatitis C, zidovudine therapy in human immunodeficiency virus infection, myelosuppressive chemotherapy of a solid malignancy, or chronic kidney disease. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Why can a normal or elevated serum ferritin fail to exclude iron deficiency anemia?","choices":["Ferritin reflects the serum iron level","Ferritin rises with microcytosis","Ferritin stays normal until late","Ferritin is an acute phase reactant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ferritin and, to a lesser degree, transferrin are acute phase reactants, so a patient with an inflammatory condition (infection, autoimmune disease) who is also iron deficient may have a normal or even elevated ferritin. A normal or elevated ferritin does not exclude iron deficiency anemia. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Pernicious anemia raises the risk of which cancer?","choices":["Gastric cancer","Colon cancer","Acute myeloid leukemia","Myelodysplastic syndrome"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pernicious anemia is an autoimmune reaction against parietal cells and/or intrinsic factor, which results in atrophy of the gastric mucosa and an increased risk of gastric cancer. A gastric biopsy may show chronic atrophic gastritis. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 29, 44, 48, 57"},{"q":"Which antibiotic is associated with aplastic anemia?","choices":["Isoniazid","Phenytoin","Metformin","Chloramphenicol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chloramphenicol is a drug associated with aplastic anemia, with cytotoxic or chemotherapeutic agents, ionizing radiation and toxins such as benzene and pesticides. Direct injury to hematopoietic stem cells is one of its pathophysiologic processes. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 29, 42, 56, 57"},{"q":"How is glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency inherited, and who is generally affected?","choices":["Autosomal dominant; both sexes","X-linked recessive; males","Autosomal recessive; both sexes","X-linked dominant; females"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency is X-linked recessive, so generally males are affected. Carrier females have two populations of red cells, one normal and one deficient, and are generally unaffected. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 76, 79, 100"},{"q":"How many alpha and beta globin genes does a person normally carry?","choices":["Four alpha, four beta","Four alpha, two beta","Two alpha, four beta","Two alpha, two beta"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. There are 4 alpha globin genes and 2 beta globin genes, and the thalassemia phenotype depends on how many are mutated: from a silent carrier with one alpha gene affected (a-/aa) to a form incompatible with life. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 86, 89"},{"q":"Which drug is the disease-modifying therapy that raises fetal hemoglobin in sickle cell disease?","choices":["Rituximab","Hydroxyurea","Penicillin","Folic acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Disease modification in sickle cell disease is hydroxyurea, which raises fetal hemoglobin (HbF) and reduces vaso-occlusive events. Prevention adds vaccinations, childhood penicillin prophylaxis and infection education. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 74, 89, 98"},{"q":"Which bilirubin pattern is expected in hemolytic anemia?","choices":["Conjugated alone is raised","Unconjugated above conjugated","Conjugated above unconjugated","Both fractions normal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both bilirubin fractions are likely elevated in hemolytic anemia, with unconjugated greater than conjugated. Macrophages break down red cells and release unconjugated bilirubin, which the liver then conjugates, so the unconjugated fraction leads. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 66, 67"},{"q":"What is the treatment of primary hemochromatosis?","choices":["Red cell transfusion","Glucocorticoids","Therapeutic phlebotomy","Iron chelation first"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Primary hemochromatosis is treated with therapeutic phlebotomy with ferritin-guided follow-up. Secondary overload is managed by treating the cause, with chelation considered when phlebotomy is unsuitable. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 74, 89, 104"},{"q":"Which medication class is singled out as a cause of iron deficiency anemia?","choices":["Antituberculosis drugs","Folate antagonists","Loop diuretics","Proton pump inhibitors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Among the dietary factors behind iron deficiency, antacids (calcium) and medications, particularly proton pump inhibitors, are listed. A patient on long-term acid suppression with a microcytic anemia should have that medication reviewed. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which medication is listed as a cause of folate deficiency?","choices":["Chloramphenicol","Proton pump inhibitors","Phenytoin","Isoniazid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Phenytoin is on the list of drugs that cause folate deficiency, with methotrexate, 5-fluorouracil, hydroxyurea and metformin. A patient taking one of these with a macrocytic anemia should have folate deficiency considered. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 10, 29, 42, 57"},{"q":"For an adolescent with heavy menses, which care priority is listed?","choices":["Screen family for iron overload","Check counts and ferritin","Use neonatal ranges","Screen for marrow disorders"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. For adolescents with heavy menses, check a complete blood count and ferritin, treat iron deficiency and control the bleeding, while supporting adherence and the transition to adult care. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"When are chelating agents used in lead poisoning?","choices":["Severe cases with neurologic effects","Any detectable lead level","Before exposure is stopped","Microcytic anemia without symptoms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of lead poisoning starts by stopping exposure and is based on the lead level; in severe cases with neurologic effects, chelating agents can be used: succimer, dimercaprol or ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid. The neurologic system is the most vulnerable. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which drug is listed as a reversible cause of sideroblastic anemia?","choices":["Omeprazole","Methotrexate","Epoetin alfa","Isoniazid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The acquired, reversible type of sideroblastic anemia follows alcohol, certain drugs (isoniazid, chlorambucil), copper deficiency, hypothermia and lead poisoning. Removing the offending agent is the first treatment. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which features help differentiate a hemolytic anemia from the typical symptoms of anemia?","choices":["Shortness of breath","Spoon-shaped nails","Jaundice and splenomegaly","Fatigue and pallor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Typical anemia symptoms are fatigue, shortness of breath, chest pain and pallor. The differentiating features of hemolysis are jaundice, splenomegaly, joint pain and hematuria, which reflect red cell breakdown products and splenic destruction. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"After exposure to an oxidant drug, about how long is the usual lag before hemolysis appears in glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency?","choices":["2-3 minutes","2-3 hours","2-3 days","8 weeks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patients have no symptoms until exposed to oxidative stress. Drug-induced hemolysis is acute and of variable severity, generally with a lag of 2 to 3 days, and then brings abdominal pain, jaundice, dark urine and splenomegaly. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Conversion of which compound requires both B12 and folate?","choices":["Methylmalonic acid","Transferrin","Transcobalamin","Homocysteine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Metabolism of homocysteine to methionine requires both B12 and folate. It is ordered with methylmalonic acid when B12 and folate levels are borderline or clinical suspicion continues. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 43, 47, 50"},{"q":"Under which condition does hemoglobin S polymerize and form the sickle shape?","choices":["Low oxygen tension","High oxygen tension","Oxidant drug exposure","Antibody coating"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sickle cell anemia comes from a mutation of a beta globin gene; hemoglobin S is two alpha globins and two mutant beta globins. Under conditions of low oxygen tension HbS can polymerize and form a sickle shape. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which sign of iron deficiency anemia is the urge to eat things that are not food?","choices":["Pica","Cheilosis","Koilonychia","Plummer-Vinson syndrome"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pica, the urge to eat things that are not food, is listed as common in iron deficiency anemia. It sits with fatigue, pallor, exertional shortness of breath, smooth tongue and the starred nail bed changes, cheilosis and Plummer-Vinson syndrome. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which absolute neutrophil count range defines moderate neutropenia?","choices":["Below 500 cells per microliter","500-999 cells per microliter","1,000-1,500 cells per microliter","1,500-2,000 cells per microliter"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Neutropenia is graded by the absolute neutrophil count: mild 1,000-1,500, moderate 500-999 and severe below 500 cells per microliter. Severity, duration and immune status predict infection risk. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"In anemia of chronic kidney disease, what does a low transferrin saturation with a normal or high ferritin indicate?","choices":["Iron-restricted erythropoiesis","Adequate iron for erythropoiesis","Iron overload","Folate deficiency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A low transferrin saturation may indicate iron-restricted erythropoiesis despite a normal or high ferritin, because inflammation and hepcidin restrict the iron available to the marrow. The treatment step is to correct the iron deficiency. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 47, 53"},{"q":"What does a serum ferritin below 30 ng/mL indicate in a patient with anemia of chronic disease?","choices":["A falsely low inflammatory value","Coexistent iron deficiency","The expected chronic disease pattern","Sideroblastic transformation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In anemia of chronic disease serum ferritin should be normal or increased; a value below 30 ng/mL indicates coexistent iron deficiency. The labs are the same as for iron deficiency, with an erythrocyte sedimentation rate and/or C-reactive protein if inflammation is not apparent. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the trade-off of taking ferrous sulfate with food?","choices":["More side effects, less absorption","Fewer side effects, less absorption","More side effects, more absorption","Fewer side effects, more absorption"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Taking ferrous sulfate with food reduces its side effects but also decreases absorption. The better-absorbed approach is on an empty stomach in the morning with a full glass of orange juice, whose low pH, citric acid and vitamin C aid uptake. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 18, 19"},{"q":"What is the listed prognosis of megaloblastic anemia once treated?","choices":["Delayed B12 care may leave deficits","Depends on transplant response","Persists while disease is active","Usually requires lifelong transfusion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With B12 or folate deficiency treated, counts improve, but delayed B12 treatment may leave neurologic deficits, which is why neurologic recovery is monitored during follow-up. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"What is the normal range for the mean corpuscular volume, the average size of red blood cells?","choices":["32-36 fL","60-80 fL","80-100 fL","100-120 fL"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The mean corpuscular volume, the average size of the red blood cells, runs 80-100 femtoliters. Below 80 fL is microcytosis, the pattern of iron deficiency; above 100 fL is macrocytosis. Reading it is one of the first steps in sorting an anemia. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 7"}]};
